WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.370
The solution was found by
another British mathematician

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.790
and cryptographer, Clifford Cox.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.460
Cox needed to construct
a special kind

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.280
of one-way function called a
trap door one-way function.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.660
This is a function that is easy
to compute in one direction,

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.250
yet difficult to
reverse unless you

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.770
have special information
called the trap door.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.350
For this, he turned to
modular exponentiation,

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.600
which we introduced
as clock arithmetic

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.060
in the Diffie-Hellman
key exchange, as follows.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.030
Take a number, raise it
to some exponent, divide

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.990
by the modulus, and
output the remainder.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.790
This can be used to encrypt
a message as follows.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:51.570
Imagine Bob has a message, which
is converted into a number, m.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.300
He then multiplies his
number by itself e times,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.890
where e is a public
exponent, then

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.180
he divides the result
by a random number n

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.720
and outputs the remainder
of the division.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.900
This results in some number c.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.680
This calculation
is easy to perform.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.320
However, given only c, e, and
n, it is much more difficult

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.210
to determine which m
was used, because we'd

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.900
have to resort to some
form of trial and error.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.900
So this is our one-way
function that we

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.090
can apply to m--
easy to perform,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.450
but difficult to reverse.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.170
It is our mathematical lock.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.530
Now, what about the key?

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:41.150
The key is the trap door,
some piece of information

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.740
that makes it easy to reverse
the encryption We need

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.370
to raise c to some
other exponent,

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:52.310
say d, which will undo the
initial operation applied to m,

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.830
and return the
original message, m.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.790
So both operations
together is the same as m

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:01.860
to the power of e all raised
to the power of d, which

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.680
is the same as m to
the power of e times d.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.380
e is the encryption.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.289
d is the decryption.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.790
Therefore we need a way
for Alice to construct e

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:17.090
and d which makes it difficult
for anyone else to find d.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:19.300
This requires a second
one-way function,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.510
which is used for generating d.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.930
And for this, he
looked back to Euclid.

